Ladybird caps each serialized history state at 16 MiB, and gives a
tab’s whole session history a 1 GiB budget for its encoded size,
enforced in the UI process. Gecko also caps each state at 16 MiB, in
nsDocShell::AddState() (the browser.history.maxStateObjectSize pref),
and throws past it. WebKit’s History::updateAndCheckStateObjectQuota()
instead allows each main-frame document 64 MB of state and URLs in
all, and throws a QuotaExceededError past that. Blink sets no limit on
a state’s size. But Blink and Gecko both bound the total by keeping at
most 50 session history entries (Blink’s kMaxSessionHistoryEntries,
and Gecko’s browser.sessionhistory.max_entries pref), which Ladybird
doesn’t do yet, so its size budget bounds the total instead.
